<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Israel</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as State of Israel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Israel is a country in the Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> region of West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, Jordan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zionism">Zionism</a>, which sought a homeland for the Jewish people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, grew from European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">antisemitism</a> in the late 19th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> established Mandatory Palestine in 1920, and in 1947 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> proposed partitioning the land between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and the Arab-majority population. Israel declared independence on 14 May 1948, and neighbouring Arab states invaded the next day. Most Palestinian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> fled or were expelled during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakba">Nakba</a>, and the Israeli–Palestinian conflict persists.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/israel.jpg" alt="Israel" width="330" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Israel Belkind and Fanny Abramovitch (original) “The Provisional Council of State Proclamation of the Flag of the State of Israel” of 25 Tis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Israel.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Evidence of archaic humans at Ubeidiya dates to around 1.5 million years ago, and the Natufian culture of about 10,000 BCE adopted settled life before agriculture. Texts from about 2000 BCE name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> and the Canaanites, organised as independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> much of Canaan was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom of Egypt</a>. Modern archaeological accounts suggest that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> and their culture branched out of the Canaanite peoples through settlement of the central highlands. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah-stele">Merneptah Stele</a>, from the late 13th century BCE, is the earliest known artefact to mention Israel as a collective.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> allowed the exiled Judean population to return after capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 539 BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> was completed about 520 BCE. Alexander the Great conquered the region in 332 BCE. Decrees of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes">Antiochus IV</a> outlawing Jewish practices triggered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabean-revolt">Maccabean Revolt</a> in 167 BCE, and the Hasmoneans had secured autonomy by 142 or 141 BCE. The Roman siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 63 BCE ended their civil war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> conquered the region in 1516.</p><p>Chaim Weizmann's efforts to win British support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zionism">Zionism</a> secured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balfour-declaration">Balfour Declaration</a> of 1917, which backed a Jewish national home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. In November 1947 the General Assembly adopted Resolution 181, recommending separate Jewish and Arab states, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> under an international regime. The Jewish leadership accepted the plan and the Arab leadership rejected it. The 1949 armistice left Israel controlling more territory than the plan had allotted, while Gaza came under Egyptian control and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a> under Jordan.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a> of 1967 Israel occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a>, Gaza, the Sinai and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golan-heights">Golan Heights</a> and annexed East Jerusalem. Following the Camp David Accords, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and Israel signed a peace treaty in 1979, and the Sinai was returned in 1982. Israel signed a peace treaty with Jordan in 1994, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo-accords">Oslo Accords</a> of 1993 introduced limited Palestinian self-rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Israel is a parliamentary republic whose Knesset is elected by proportional representation; the president is largely ceremonial. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> is the seat of government and the proclaimed capital, though East Jerusalem's status is internationally disputed, and Tel Aviv is the largest urban area. The population was about 10 million in 2025, of whom 72 per cent were recorded as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and 21 per cent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>. The White City of Tel Aviv is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> heritage site, and the Israel Museum in Jerusalem holds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea-scrolls">Dead Sea Scrolls</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Israel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its outcome shaped the region: the <b>State of Israel</b>, the West Bank annexed by Transjordan, Gaza under Egypt, and the 1948 Palestinian expulsion and flight.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mandatory-palestine">Mandatory Palestine · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1948 the <b>State of Israel</b> was founded; Jews from Arab countries, from Europe and later from the Soviet Union and Ethiopia settled there.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The British Mandate recognised Hebrew as an official language in 1922, and in 1948 it became the main language of the new <b>State of Israel</b>, where immigrants from around the world learned it in intensive courses.</blockquote><a href="/language/hebrew">Hebrew · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>On 14 May 1948 Ben-Gurion declared the <b>State of Israel</b>, and neighbouring Arab states joined the war.</blockquote><a href="/movement/zionism">Zionism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>When the <b>State of Israel</b> was established in 1948, &quot;Hatikvah&quot; was proclaimed its national anthem unofficially.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hatikvah">Hatikvah</a></li></ul></section>
